edit('def-complex-domain',lambda s:s.replace('Open means open in the modulus metric of [[def-metric-topology]], and connected has the meaning of [[def-connected-space]]. By [[rem-complex-plane-euclidean-dictionary]], these are exactly the usual Euclidean notions for the corresponding subset of $\\mathbb R^2$.','Open means open for $d_{\\mathbb C}$ of [[def-complex-metric-convergence-and-continuity]] in the metric topology of [[def-metric-topology]] (a topology by [[thm-metric-open-set-algebra]]). Connected has the meaning of [[def-connected-space]] for this topology and its subspace topology on $U$.'))
edit('def-oriented-complex-triangle-and-boundary',lambda s:s.replace('by [[cor-piecewise-c1-paths-have-additive-speed-integral-length]].','by [[thm-complex-path-coordinate-length-rectifiability-and-piecewise-c1-speed]]: its coordinate path is affine with constant derivative $\\Phi(v)-\\Phi(u)$, whose Euclidean norm is $|v-u|$.').replace('under the standard identification of [[rem-complex-plane-euclidean-dictionary]]','for $d_{\\mathbb C}(z,w)=|z-w|$ of [[def-complex-metric-convergence-and-continuity]]').replace('Its perimeter is the length of its boundary contour, including in the degenerate cases.','Its perimeter is the length of its boundary contour, including in the degenerate cases: each affine concatenation piece has its edge length by the complex speed-integral theorem, and the sum of those integrals is the sum of the three edge lengths. Zero edges contribute zero.'))
edit('def-complex-contours-reversal-concatenation-and-closedness',lambda s:s[:s.index('## Definition')]+r'''## Definition

A **complex contour** is a continuous rectifiable path $\gamma:[a,b]\to\mathbb C$, $a\le b$, in the sense of [[def-complex-path-polygonal-length-and-rectifiability]]. It is **closed** when $\gamma(a)=\gamma(b)$. Its **reversal** is $\gamma^-(t)=\gamma(a+b-t)$ on the same interval.

If complex contours $\alpha,\beta:[0,1]\to\mathbb C$ satisfy $\alpha(1)=\beta(0)$, define

$$ (\alpha*\beta)(t)=\begin{cases}\alpha(2t),&0\le t\le\tfrac12,\\\beta(2t-1),&\tfrac12\le t\le1.\end{cases} $$

Endpoint matching makes the two expressions agree at $1/2$ and makes the map continuous there. The concatenation is rectifiable and its length is $L_{\mathbb C}(\alpha)+L_{\mathbb C}(\beta)$: inserting $1/2$ into a partition can only increase its sum by the triangle inequality; the refined sum splits into two sums of the factors, while combining partitions from both factors gives the reverse supremum inequality. Taking factor sums within $\varepsilon/2$ of their finite suprema proves equality. This argument includes constant pieces. Reversal preserves rectifiability and length by [[thm-complex-path-length-invariant-under-monotone-reparametrization]].

A complex contour is **piecewise $C^1$** when its coordinate path is continuous piecewise $C^1$ as specified in [[thm-complex-path-coordinate-length-rectifiability-and-piecewise-c1-speed]]; its length is the speed integral supplied there, including corners and zero-speed pieces. Singleton paths have length zero.

An increasing continuous bijection of compact parameter intervals preserves orientation; a decreasing one reverses orientation, using the order convention of [[def-monotone-function]]. The length is unchanged by either, and more generally by every continuous surjective nondecreasing or nonincreasing reparametrization, including pauses, by [[thm-complex-path-length-invariant-under-monotone-reparametrization]].
''')
edit('def-wirtinger-derivatives',lambda s:s[:s.index('## Definition')]+r'''## Definition

Let $U\subseteq\mathbb C$ be open for $d_{\mathbb C}$ ([[def-complex-metric-convergence-and-continuity]]). Put $V=\Phi[U]$, where $\Phi(a+bi)=(a,b)$ is the bijection of [[thm-complex-numbers-are-the-real-coordinate-plane]]. Then $V$ is open by [[thm-complex-coordinate-isometry-and-topology-transport]]. For $f:U\to\mathbb C$ define $\widetilde f=\Phi\circ f\circ\Phi^{-1}:V\to\mathbb R^2$, with component functions $u=\operatorname{Re}(f\circ\Phi^{-1})$ and $v=\operatorname{Im}(f\circ\Phi^{-1})$ ([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]). All real partial derivatives below are those of $\widetilde f$ on $V$ in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], evaluated at the coordinate point in question. Write $\partial_x f=u_x+iv_x$ and $\partial_y f=u_y+iv_y$ after transport back to $U$.

At a point where the four real partial derivatives exist, define

$$ \partial_z f:=\frac12(\partial_x f-i\partial_y f)=\frac12(u_x+v_y)+\frac{i}{2}(v_x-u_y), $$

$$ \partial_{\bar z}f:=\frac12(\partial_x f+i\partial_y f)=\frac12(u_x-v_y)+\frac{i}{2}(v_x+u_y). $$

Real total differentiability means total differentiability of $\widetilde f$ under [[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]], with a real-linear derivative as in [[def-euclidean-linear-map]]. Its transported real-linear map $Df$ is supplied by [[thm-complex-coordinate-map-preserves-real-total-differentiability]]. If $f$ is real totally differentiable, put $A=\partial_x f$ and $B=\partial_y f$. The remainder definition along the two coordinate axes gives $Df(1)=A$ and $Df(i)=B$. Real linearity then gives $Df(s+it)=sA+tB$ for real $s,t$, whereas direct expansion of $\frac12(A-iB)(s+it)+\frac12(A+iB)(s-it)$ gives the same expression. Thus

$$ Df(h)=(\partial_z f)h+(\partial_{\bar z}f)\overline h. $$

A real-differentiable map is **antiholomorphic** on $U$ when $\partial_z f=0$ at every point of $U$. Thus its differential is conjugate-linear at every point; no complex differentiability is asserted unless the other Wirtinger derivative also vanishes.
''')
edit('prop-triangle-integrals-give-a-primitive-on-a-star-shaped-domain',lambda s:s.replace('in the sense of [[rem-plane-star-shaped-and-convex-dictionary]]','meaning that $U$ is open for $d_{\\mathbb C}$ and $\\Phi[U]$ is star-shaped with respect to $\\Phi(a)$ in [[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]], using [[thm-complex-coordinate-isometry-and-topology-transport]]').replace('**Given:** An open set $U$ star-shaped with respect to $a$,','**Given:** A set $U$ open for $d_{\\mathbb C}$ with $\\Phi[U]$ star-shaped with respect to $\\Phi(a)$,').replace('Since $U$ is open, choose','The coordinate topology and affine-segment transport of [[thm-complex-coordinate-isometry-and-topology-transport]] give $(1-t)a+tw\\in U$ for $w\\in U$ and $0\\le t\\le1$. Since $U$ is open, choose'))
edit('lem-local-holomorphic-logarithm-nonvanishing-function-on-disc',lambda s:s.replace('if $D(a,r)$ is an open disc with $r>0$','if $D(a,r)=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z-a|<r\\}$ with $r>0$').replace('**Given:** A disc $D(a,r)$ with $r>0$ and a nowhere-zero holomorphic function $h$ on it.','**Given:** A disc $D(a,r)=\\{z:|z-a|<r\\}$ with $r>0$ and a nowhere-zero holomorphic function $h$ on it. It is nonempty since it contains $a$. For each $z$ in it, $\\varepsilon=r-|z-a|>0$ and $|w-z|<\\varepsilon$ imply $|w-a|<r$ by the triangle inequality, so the disc is open in $d_{\\mathbb C}$.').replace('so every segment between two points of the disc stays in it; taking $z=a$ makes the disc star-shaped with respect to $a$ in the sense of [[rem-plane-star-shaped-and-convex-dictionary]] and [[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]], and [L4] makes the disc a connected, hence a complex, domain.','so every segment between two points of the disc stays in it. By affine-segment and openness transport in [[thm-complex-coordinate-isometry-and-topology-transport]], its coordinate image is a convex open subset of $\\mathbb R^2$ in [[def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space]], hence star-shaped at $\\Phi(a)$ in [[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]. Each coordinate segment is a continuous path by [L4]; composing it with the coordinate homeomorphism inverse gives a continuous path in the disc. Thus the disc is path connected, hence connected by [L4]; together with nonemptiness and openness this makes it a complex domain in [[def-complex-domain]].'))
